  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2003/11/swrl#Variable" ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.semanticweb.org/209332175/ontologies/2024/003/LFTDO#wandering",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2003/11/swrl#Variable" ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing",
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment" : [ {
    "@value" : "A 'thing' represents the Livestock Farming Theft Detection Ontology(LFTDO).\nThe purpose of Livestock Farming Theft Detection Ontology(LFTDO) is to integrate and analyze data from various sources, such as sensor networks, surveillance cameras, and databases, to identify suspicious activities that may indicate theft in a farming area. Furthermore, it enables livestock theft detection and prevention systems and applications to be accessible, flexible, transparent, interoperable, scalable, and cost effective.  \nThe aim is to assist with the early detection of livestock theft by providing a structured and standardized way of representing knowledge about livestock farming, including: \n1.Types of livestock\n\n2.The vital components of livestock farming include ownership, farming areas, grazing areas, water sources, and technological resources (i.e. IoT devices, gateways, cloud computing, and machine-learning algorithms).\n\n3.A variety of activities happening such as livestock movement, grazing, and feeding.\n\n4.The people involved in livestock farming include farmers, extended-farmer officers, law enforcement agencies, and veterinarians.\n\n5.A variety of events occurs in a farming area such as livestock theft   \nLFTDO standardizes the data structures, integration, and knowledge representation of livestock farming by defining the concepts, entities, relationships, and rules governing them. \n\nThe ontology should be able to reason livestock behaviour patterns and environmental conditions data provided to infer the possibility of livestock theft to alert livestock farmers and other stakeholders.\n\nMain Entities:\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1.Actor\n2.Behaviour\n3.BehaviourState\n4.CloudService\n5.Device\n6.EnvironmentaCondition\n7.Livestock\n8.Locatio\n9.HealthCondition"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#topDataProperty",
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "topDataProperty"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#topObjectProperty",
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "owl:topObjectProperty"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.w3.org/2003/11/swrlb#divide",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2003/11/swrl#Builtin" ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.w3.org/2003/11/swrlb#equal",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2003/11/swrl#Builtin" ]
}, {
  "@id" : "http://www.w3.org/2003/11/swrlb#greaterThan",
